Z||000003||Will||01-10-2001||09:50 AM||||For all three of these solutions (and to use the ball joint tool, duh), you need to loosen the ball joint nut until the top of the nut is flush with or higher than the top of the ball joint shank.<P>Option 1: you may need a helper for this. Put a heavy hammer against one side of the place where the ball joint shank goes through. Hit the other side HARD with another heavy hammer. This will momentarily deform the taper, popping the ball joint out.<BR>The problem is that the Fiero suspension is cramped, and you will need to use pieces of bar stock as punches for this operation.<P>Option 2: use a pickle fork. This will destroy your old ball joint boots, however.<P>Option 3: Using a LARGE punch and heavy hammer smack the ball joint out. Put the punch on the top of the ball joint shank and pound it straight down. You will need ot hit it HARD.<P>Option 3 is a last resort and option 1 is hard to accomplish. Option 2 is the most predictble and consistent method.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||01-10-2001||11:07 AM||||I actually BENT a pickle fork...<BR>Currrently the entire assemblies are off the car and they are going to my machine shop (where I work).  I figure some kloogy press work, or some really big inertial persuaders will have to be done.<BR>THANKS ALL and keep the idea flowing...<BR>Must get done by saturday!!! First race of season on Sunday.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||01-10-2001||11:44 AM||||Can we can assume you loosened the nuts a few turns first?||||reg||5
Z||000006||Greg Piet||01-10-2001||12:43 PM||||oh yeah the castle nuts are off.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||01-10-2001||04:07 PM||||Put the castle nuts back on upside down until they are flush with the end of the ball joint shank, then try beating them to death. You may want to support the hub on a bottle jack and a 2x4 block or something similar if you use the inertial persuader method.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||01-10-2001||04:29 PM||||HUM . . . . . <P>That's sort of strange . . . .  I just finished up doing an upper (stock rivits) and a lower (different sides) on an '87! Total time 1.5 start to finish and out the door!<BR>Sure your using the correct size pickle sticker?||||reg||1
Z||000009||william_thorniii||01-10-2001||04:45 PM||||Greg,<P>I feel for 'ya man.  I spent more time trying to get my frt. pass. side ball joint off than I did dropping the entire cradle and driver's side front suspension.  I finally got the thing off with a BJ press driven with a 1/2 breaker bar.  It mushroomed the BJ so badly that I definitely need to replace it before I hit the road. <P>Oh yeah, I forgot to mention that I also spent many hours pounding 2 pickle forks in there trying to break that damn thing loose.  The entire time I was using a 20# sledge... the answer is obvious I needed a B'rFH!<P>William T. Thorn, III<p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000010||lowCG||01-10-2001||08:27 PM||||I always try a few good whacks perpendicular to the stud,like Will wrote,at the very least it'll loosen them a bit.Also,a really good penetrating solution like Maltby's helps also.Good luck man!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Greg Piet||01-11-2001||12:14 AM||||Yes for the record I have touched 95% of all nuts bolts on my car, and the ball joints were absolutely without a doubt the worst!<BR>I got em off finally, by machining a GM seperator tool...   1 hour manufacturing time and 1 hour to remove them...<BR>I would put a bubck of force on the tool, and nothing... let it sit for 5 minutes under strain conected to the vice then POING!.<P>I have pulled a cradle much faster than these friggin ball joints...  I DONT WANNA DO THIS AGAIN!||||reg||1
